Simon Tatham schrieb:
> 
> Jakob Creutzig  <creutzig@mathematik.tu-darmstadt.de> wrote:
> > On the other hand, it might be true that the human body is capable
> > of creating more impulse when throwing heavy objects.
> 
> I'd guess so, because you're limited by how far and how fast you can
> move your arm during the throwing action.

How exactly you move the arm is also a factor - i'd use a much
different technique throwing a tennis ball (more having the arm flail
around the shoulder joint) than a bowling ball (more of a straight
outward push). Particularly unwieldy objects would probably end up
moving with a much lower velocity than particularly 'handy' items like
a hammer or hatchet.

> If you throw a heavy suit
> of armour, you're accelerating it as hard as you can for the time it
> takes to travel a distance of (say) a metre.

I think a suit of armour would be an extremely awkward item to throw -
it's not a compact mass, but rather has a great many joints and no
real 'centre' against which to apply force to make it move as one;
much of the force used will be wasted by internal movements in the
item. 

> If you `threw' an object by hanging it in the air in front of you
> and smacking it sharply with a single sharp blow, _then_

... you'd break your fingers <g>

> I might
> believe that the impulse you imparted was roughly equivalent for all
> kinds of object.

Theoretically, perhaps. In practice, it still wouldn't be delivered as
a single impulse and deformation of the item would play a role.
How about bracing your _legs_ against a (floating) chest and pushing
off it? Should allow bringing a lot more force to bear than just about
everything you can achieve with your arms.

